From 5eba4d6e0133da4daa96041393082adfe2f8a7d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fredrik Hult <fredrik.hult@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 20:28:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] ReflowCursorLine: the wrapped line holding the cursor can
 reflow too

Off by default, which keeps xterm.js's rule: the cursor's wrapped group is left to the
application's SIGWINCH redraw and cut to the new width. Behind Windows' ConPTY nothing redraws
it -- ConPTY reflows its own copy of the screen, as Windows Terminal does, and repaints nothing
-- so a narrowed prompt stayed cut and the next echo landed where ConPTY's cursor was rather
than where this one had been left.

With the option on, the cursor is held as its group's first line object and a cell offset into
the group, both of which survive reflow in either direction, and put back after every other
clamp. ReflowSmaller's pop assumed the bottom row was blank, which only held while the cursor's
group was skipped; it now scrolls instead when the bottom row has content.

Tests cover the option off and on, a cursor in the middle of its group, a group at the bottom
of a full screen, a scrolled buffer, a wide character at the wrap point, the alternate screen,
and the three commits before this one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
